\section{Repeat-group templates}{

Household surveys nest children inside caregivers inside households, and wide
exports encode that nesting in the column name. Concepts at the \code{"child"} and
\code{"vaccine"} levels therefore accept templates with \code{{}} placeholders:
\itemize{
\item \code{{c}} -- caregiver (or first-level repeat) index, 1..\code{n_caregivers}
\item \code{{k}} -- child (or second-level repeat) index, 1..\code{n_children}
\item \code{{v}} -- vaccine index from the schedule, unpadded (\code{1}, \code{2}, ...)
\item \code{{vv}} -- vaccine index zero-padded to two digits (\code{01}, \code{02}, ...)
\item \code{{vaccine}} -- vaccine name from the schedule (\code{"BCG"}, \code{"PENTA1"}, ...)
\item \code{{antigen}} -- antigen series from the schedule (\code{"PENTA"} for \code{PENTA1},
\code{PENTA2} and \code{PENTA3}), for concepts collected once per series such as
\code{recall_ever} and \code{recall_count}
}

For example \code{card_date = "CVH{vv}_date_{c}_{k}"} resolves to
\code{CVH04_date_1_2} for the fourth vaccine of the second child of the first
caregiver. A concept given without placeholders is treated as a single
column, which is what long-format (one row per child) exports need.
}

